# Strategy Design Pattern (Interview Notes)

## Definition

The **Strategy Pattern** is a **behavioral design pattern** that defines a family of algorithms,
encapsulates each algorithm in a separate class, and makes them interchangeable. 
The Context can switch between algorithms **at runtime** without modifying its own code.

**Key Idea:**

> **Encapsulate what varies and program to an interface, not an implementation.**

---

# Interview Scenario

### Payment Processing System

An e-commerce application supports multiple payment methods such as:

* Credit Card
* PayPal
* Bank Transfer
* UPI
* Apple Pay

The payment algorithm depends on the payment method selected by the user.

---

# Without Strategy Pattern

All payment logic is placed inside a single class using multiple **if-else** statements.

```cpp
class PaymentProcessor
{
public:
    void processPayment(string paymentMethod, int amount)
    {
        if(paymentMethod == "CreditCard")
        {
            // Credit Card logic
        }
        else if(paymentMethod == "PayPal")
        {
            // PayPal logic
        }
        else if(paymentMethod == "BankTransfer")
        {
            // Bank Transfer logic
        }
    }
};
```

---

## Problems

### 1. Violates Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Whenever a new payment method is added (UPI, Apple Pay, Crypto, etc.), the existing `processPayment()` function must be modified.

```cpp
if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
else if(...)
...
```

The class keeps growing with every new payment option.

---

### 2. Difficult to Maintain

As the number of payment methods increases, the function becomes large and difficult to understand.

```
Credit Card
PayPal
UPI
Bank Transfer
Apple Pay
Crypto
Net Banking
Wallet
```

Everything is handled inside one function.

---

### 3. Tightly Coupled Code

The `PaymentProcessor` knows the implementation details of every payment method.

Changing one payment algorithm may accidentally affect others.

---

### 4. Poor Extensibility

Adding a new payment method requires modifying already tested code, increasing the risk of introducing bugs.

---

### 5. Hard to Unit Test

Each payment algorithm cannot be tested independently because all logic resides in one function.

---

# Using Strategy Pattern

## Step 1: Strategy Interface

```cpp
class PaymentStrategy
{
public:
    virtual ~PaymentStrategy() = default;
    virtual void pay(int amount) = 0;
};
```

---

## Step 2: Concrete Strategy Classes

```cpp
class CreditCardPayment : public PaymentStrategy
{
public:
    void pay(int amount) override
    {
        cout << "Processing payment using Credit Card\n";
    }
};

class PayPalPayment : public PaymentStrategy
{
public:
    void pay(int amount) override
    {
        cout << "Processing payment using PayPal\n";
    }
};

class BankTransferPayment : public PaymentStrategy
{
public:
    void pay(int amount) override
    {
        cout << "Processing payment using Bank Transfer\n";
    }
};
```

Each class implements its own payment algorithm.

---

## Step 3: Context Class

The Context delegates payment processing to the selected strategy.

```cpp
class PaymentProcessor
{
private:
    PaymentStrategy* paymentStrategy;

public:
    PaymentProcessor(PaymentStrategy* strategy)
        : paymentStrategy(strategy)
    {
    }

    ~PaymentProcessor()
    {
        delete paymentStrategy;
    }

    void setPaymentStrategy(PaymentStrategy* strategy)
    {
        delete paymentStrategy;
        paymentStrategy = strategy;
    }

    void processPayment(int amount)
    {
        if(paymentStrategy)
        {
            paymentStrategy->pay(amount);
        }
    }
};
```

---

## Client Code

```cpp
PaymentProcessor processor(new CreditCardPayment());

processor.processPayment(100);

processor.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment());

processor.processPayment(200);

processor.setPaymentStrategy(new BankTransferPayment());

processor.processPayment(300);
```

The payment algorithm changes **at runtime** without modifying the `PaymentProcessor`.

---

# Class Diagram

```
                 PaymentProcessor
                        |
                        | has-a
                        |
                        V
                PaymentStrategy
                      ^
        -------------------------------
        |             |              |
 CreditCard     PayPalPayment   BankTransfer
```

---

# Advantages

### ✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle

To add a new payment method, simply create a new strategy.

```cpp
class UpiPayment : public PaymentStrategy
{
public:
    void pay(int amount) override
    {
        cout << "UPI Payment\n";
    }
};
```

No existing code needs modification.

---

### ✔ Runtime Flexibility

The payment algorithm can be changed dynamically.

```cpp
processor.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment());
```

No changes are required in the `PaymentProcessor`.

---

### ✔ Loose Coupling

The `PaymentProcessor` depends only on the `PaymentStrategy` interface.

It does not know the implementation details of individual payment methods.

---

### ✔ Easy to Extend

Adding new payment methods does not affect existing strategy classes.

---

### ✔ Easy to Test

Each payment strategy can be unit tested independently.

```
CreditCardPayment

PayPalPayment

BankTransferPayment
```

---

### ✔ Better Maintainability

Each class has a single responsibility, making the code cleaner and easier to maintain.

---

# Interview Summary (30 Seconds)

> The Strategy Pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each algorithm into a 
separate class, and makes them interchangeable. Instead of writing long if-else chains, 
the Context delegates the work to a Strategy object. This follows the Open/Closed Principle, 
supports runtime behavior changes, reduces coupling, improves maintainability, and allows each algorithm to be tested independently.

---

# Real-World Examples

* Payment Gateway (Credit Card, PayPal, UPI, Apple Pay)
* Compression Algorithms (ZIP, RAR, 7Z)
* Route Planning (Car, Bike, Walking)
* Sorting Algorithms (Quick Sort, Merge Sort, Heap Sort)
* Discount Calculation (Festival, Student, Premium)
* Tax Calculation (India, US, UK)
* Authentication (OAuth, LDAP, JWT)
* Image Compression (JPEG, PNG, WebP)

---

# Key Interview Points

* **Design Pattern:** Behavioral
* **Purpose:** Encapsulate interchangeable algorithms.
* **Relationship:** Context **has-a** Strategy.
* **Polymorphism:** Runtime polymorphism.
* **SOLID Principle:** Open/Closed Principle (OCP).
* **Memory Management:** When using raw pointers, ensure proper ownership by deleting the previous 
strategy before assigning a new one and declaring the base class destructor as `virtual`.
